en you break the clock these  clock regulated outputs are  desynchronized so  leaf movement might have a shorter  period than 24 hours  it might have a longer period or as  shown here it might desynchronize to the  point of arrhythmicity where you  lose rhythms altogether and the clock  controls more than just leaf movement of  course  so cold tolerance flowering time even  biotic stress response and pest  resistance  is clock regulated so all of these  phenotypes are actually  have a time of day component associated  with them  so this um figure  uh represents the core oscillator in  arabidopsis  and so it consists of these interlocking  transcription translation feedback loops  and so i'm not going to walk you through  the very complex signaling pathway  uh pictured here but um what i want to  demonstrate  is that this complex signaling pathway  operates to produce these very precisely  timed  phased patterns of gene expression over  the course of the day  and it's this time of day specific gene  expression  that produces these time of day specific  phenotypes  and one thing about the clock oscillator  is that all of these components  have been identified by forward genetics  and so in the new snow lab we take  a proteomic approach to identify new  circadian clock components  and even connections between  environmental input pathways  and physiological output pathways  and the way we do this is using a method  called affinity purification coupled  with mass spectrometry  and so in this method we're using  arabidopsis  where we've engineered it with a clock  gene of interest  driven by its endogenous promoter and we  add two affinity tags  a 6x histidine tag and a 3x flag tag and  you might hear me refer to this as the  hfc tag  for his flag c terminal and we  engineer this in a clock mutant  background so whatever  gene we're interested in we're  engineering that into the clock mutant  background so our affinity tag version  is the only one  operating in that plan